package Vixen.Shaders.PostFx

import Vixen.Shaders.Core

// Auto-exposure: the post-process that has to be compute, and the only one in the library that does.
//
// Every other effect here is a fullscreen triangle writing one target, because that is what a
// fragment stage can do. This one cannot be written that way, and the reason is worth stating
// precisely: **its output is not an image.** It reduces the scene to a single number and leaves it in
// a buffer that the *next frame's* `Tonemap` binds as an ordinary uniform. A fragment stage has no
// way to write a buffer at all — it writes the render targets bound to it and nothing else — so the
// pass either runs as compute or the readback goes through the host and costs a frame of latency and
// a pipeline stall.
//
// Five entry-point shapes in one shader, chosen by `[Permutation] Mode`, because they are dispatches
// of two algorithms that share their adaptation and their bounds:
//
//   Reduce   one invocation per output texel; averages a 2×2 block of the input's log luminance.
//            Run repeatedly, each dispatch halving the image, until it is 1×1.
//   Adapt    one invocation total; reads that last texel and eases the stored exposure toward it.
//   Clear    one invocation per bin; zeroes the histogram.
//   Build    one invocation per source texel; adds a metering weight to the bin its luminance falls in.
//   Resolve  one invocation total; takes the mean of the bins between two percentiles, and adapts.
//
// **The chain and the histogram answer different questions, and the second is the one UE calls
// "Histogram" and this file used to be missing.** A geometric mean over the whole frame is a good
// number for a scene of roughly uniform brightness and a bad one for the two cases exposure exists
// for: a dark room with a bright window, and a bright street with a dark doorway. In both, the mean
// sits between the two populations and exposes for neither. Discarding the darkest and brightest
// percentiles throws away the window and the doorway and exposes for what is left, which is what a
// spot meter does and what a person's eye does.
//
// **Log luminance, not luminance.** Averaging luminance directly lets one specular highlight drag the
// whole frame's exposure down, because the arithmetic mean of a distribution with a long bright tail
// sits far above its bulk. Averaging the log and exponentiating gives the geometric mean, which is
// what "the middle of this scene's brightness" actually means and is why every implementation of this
// does it. The `log` happens once in the first reduction; every later one is a plain average of
// values that are already logarithmic.
//
// **Adaptation is a rate, not a blend factor.** `1 - exp(-dt * rate)` is the fraction of the way to
// the target that a given elapsed time covers, which makes the eye's response independent of frame
// rate. A raw lerp factor per frame makes a scene adapt twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60, which shows
// up as "the exposure feels wrong on my machine".

shader AutoExposure {
    /// 0 reduce one mip step, 1 adapt from the reduction, 2 clear the histogram, 3 build it, 4 resolve it.
    [Permutation] val Mode: int = 0

    /// Whether this is the first reduction, and so the one that takes the log.
    ///
    /// A permutation rather than a uniform because it removes the branch *and* the colour-space
    /// arithmetic from every later step in the chain, which is most of them.
    [Permutation] val FirstStep: bool = true

    /// The image being reduced: the scene for the first step, the previous step's output after that.
    var source: Texture2D
    var sourceSampler: Sampler

    /// Where this step writes. Half the source's size in each dimension.
    ///
    /// A storage image rather than a render target, and not only because compute cannot write one:
    /// the chain reads its own previous output, so binding it as a target would mean a render pass
    /// per halving step. `r32f` because the value is one channel and precision matters more than
    /// footprint here — the whole chain is a few hundred kilobytes.
    [Format("r32f")] var target: RWTexture2D<float4>

    /// The 1×1 result of the last reduction, which `Adapt` reads.
    [Format("r32f")] var average: RWTexture2D<float4>

    /// The exposure the tonemapper will use, kept across frames. One element.
    ///
    /// The reason this shader exists: a value that survives from one frame to the next, produced on
    /// the GPU and consumed on the GPU, without the host ever seeing it.
    var exposure: RWBuffer<float>

    var deltaTime: float = 0.016f

    /// How fast the eye adapts, in e-folds per second. Real eyes are asymmetric — dark adaptation is
    /// far slower than light — which is why there are two.
    var brightenRate: float = 3f

    var darkenRate: float = 1f

    /// The luminance a correctly exposed mid-grey sits at. 0.18 is the photographic convention.
    var middleGrey: float = 0.18f

    /// Bounds on the result, so a nearly black or blown-out frame cannot drive exposure to infinity.
    ///
    /// ⚠ **These are linear multipliers, so the floor bounds the *brightest* scene, and it has to
    /// reach daylight.** `1 / (1.2 * 2^17)` is EV 17; a sunlit surface meters at about EV 15. The
    /// value here was 0.03 — EV 4.8, a dim interior — and every scene lit in real photometric units
    /// asked for ten stops less exposure than it was allowed, settled on the clamp, and tonemapped to
    /// flat white. A meter that converges on its own bound reports nothing wrong.
    var minimumExposure: float = 0.0000063578f

    var maximumExposure: float = 8f

    /// The histogram's bins, one dispatch's worth of the frame's luminance distribution.
    ///
    /// ⚠ **Bin 0 is not a bin, it is the floor.** Everything at or below `minimumLogLuminance` lands
    /// in it, and `Resolve` skips it — because a frame with a large area of true black would otherwise
    /// have its median dragged into the floor and expose the whole scene for the black. That is the
    /// difference between a histogram meter and an average, and dropping it here gives back the
    /// average with extra steps.
    var histogram: RWBuffer<uint>

    /// The darkest luminance the histogram resolves, as a base-2 log.
    ///
    /// −10 is about a thousandth of a candela per square metre, which is a moonless night. Everything
    /// below is the floor bin.
    var minimumLogLuminance: float = -10f

    /// And the brightest. 20 is a million cd/m², which is past a clear sky beside the sun.
    var maximumLogLuminance: float = 20f

    /// The fraction of the frame discarded from the dark end before the mean is taken.
    ///
    /// ⚠ This is the whole reason the histogram exists. A geometric mean cannot ignore a dark doorway
    /// or a bright window; a percentile can, because it is a rank rather than a sum.
    var lowPercentile: float = 0.5f

    /// And from the bright end.
    var highPercentile: float = 0.95f

    /// How strongly the centre of the frame counts for more than its edge.
    ///
    /// Zero meters the whole frame evenly. Higher exponents narrow the weight toward the middle, which
    /// is what a camera's centre-weighted mode does and what stops a bright sky at the top of the frame
    /// underexposing the subject in the middle of it.
    ///
    /// ⚠ Procedural rather than a mask texture, which is what Unreal takes. A mask is more expressive
    /// and needs a texture somebody authored per scene; this is one number and covers the case anybody
    /// actually reaches for.
    var meteringPower: float = 1f

    [ComputeShader(8, 8, 1)]
    func Main([Semantic("SV_DispatchThreadID")] id: uint3) {
        if (Mode == 0) {
            Reduce(int2(int(id.x), int(id.y)))
            return
        }

        if (Mode == 2) {
            Clear(int(id.y) * 8 + int(id.x))
            return
        }

        if (Mode == 3) {
            Accumulate(int2(int(id.x), int(id.y)))
            return
        }

        // One invocation does the adaptation, and the dispatch is one group — so every other
        // invocation in that group has to be sent home. Without the test, sixty-four invocations
        // read and write the same buffer element with no ordering between them.
        if (id.x == 0u && id.y == 0u && id.z == 0u) {
            if (Mode == 4) {
                Resolve()
            } else {
                Adapt()
            }
        }
    }

    /// How many bins the histogram has. One group of 8×8 clears it, which is what fixes the number.
    static func BinCount(): int => 64

    /// Zeroes one bin.
    ///
    /// ⚠ A dispatch of its own rather than the first thing `Build` does. A build invocation cannot
    /// clear "its" bin, because a bin belongs to a luminance rather than to a pixel and every
    /// invocation in the dispatch is racing every other one for all of them. The only ordering a
    /// device offers between "all writes finished" and "all reads begin" is a dispatch boundary.
    func Clear(bin: int) {
        if (bin < BinCount() && bin < histogram.Length) {
            histogram[bin] = 0u
        }
    }

    /// Adds one texel's metering weight to the bin its luminance falls in.
    func Accumulate(coord: int2) {
        val size = target.GetDimensions()

        if (coord.x >= size.x || coord.y >= size.y || histogram.Length < BinCount()) {
            return
        }

        val centre = float2(float(coord.x) + 0.5f, float(coord.y) + 0.5f)
        val uv = centre / float2(float(size.x), float(size.y))
        val luminance = ColorSpaces.Luminance(source.SampleLevel(sourceSampler, uv, 0f).rgb)

        // ⚠ Quantised into an integer because a device's guaranteed atomic is an integer one. The
        // scale cancels in `Resolve`, which takes a ratio of sums — so 256 is a precision choice and
        // not a unit. Too small and a centre weight of 0.4 rounds to nothing; too large and a 4K
        // frame can overflow a bin.
        val weight = uint(max(Weight(uv), 0f) * 256f)

        if (weight > 0u) {
            val old = atomicAdd(histogram[BinOf(luminance)], weight)
        }
    }

    /// Which bin a luminance belongs in, with everything below the floor in bin 0.
    func BinOf(luminance: float): int {
        if (luminance <= 0f) {
            return 0
        }

        val logLuminance = log2(luminance)

        if (logLuminance <= minimumLogLuminance) {
            return 0
        }

        val span = max(maximumLogLuminance - minimumLogLuminance, Const.Epsilon)
        val position = saturate((logLuminance - minimumLogLuminance) / span)

        // 1..BinCount-1, because bin 0 is the floor and is not part of the range.
        return clamp(1 + int(position * float(BinCount() - 2)), 1, BinCount() - 1)
    }

    /// How much this part of the frame counts, centre-weighted.
    func Weight(uv: float2): float {
        if (meteringPower <= 0f) {
            return 1f
        }

        // The distance from the middle, normalised so a corner is one. `1 - d²` falls off smoothly
        // and never reaches zero at the edge, which matters: a weight of exactly zero at the border
        // makes a subject walking off screen change the exposure discontinuously.
        val offset = (uv - float2(0.5f, 0.5f)) * 2f
        val distance = saturate(length(offset) * 0.70710678f)

        return pow(max(1f - distance * distance, 0.05f), meteringPower)
    }

    /// Takes the mean of the bins between the two percentiles, and eases the exposure toward it.
    func Resolve() {
        if (exposure.Length < 1 || histogram.Length < BinCount()) {
            return
        }

        // ⚠ Bin 0 is excluded from the total as well as from the mean. Counting the floor in the
        // total but not in the sum would make a frame that is mostly black look as though its
        // percentile window covered almost nothing, and the window would collapse onto one bin.
        var total = 0f

        for (i in 1 .. 63) {
            total += float(histogram[i])
        }

        if (total <= 0f) {
            return
        }

        val low = total * saturate(lowPercentile)
        val high = total * saturate(max(highPercentile, lowPercentile))

        var seen = 0f
        var weighted = 0f
        var counted = 0f

        for (i in 1 .. 63) {
            val bin = float(histogram[i])
            val start = seen
            seen += bin

            // How much of this bin lies inside the window. Partial rather than all-or-nothing,
            // because a 64-bin histogram of a 512² frame routinely has a single bin spanning the
            // whole window — and an all-or-nothing test on that frame selects nothing at all.
            val inside = max(min(seen, high) - max(start, low), 0f)

            if (inside > 0f) {
                weighted += inside * BinLuminance(i)
                counted += inside
            }
        }

        if (counted <= 0f) {
            return
        }

        Ease(exp2(weighted / counted))
    }

    /// The log luminance a bin's centre represents.
    func BinLuminance(bin: int): float {
        val span = maximumLogLuminance - minimumLogLuminance
        val position = (float(bin - 1) + 0.5f) / float(BinCount() - 2)

        return minimumLogLuminance + position * span
    }

    /// Averages a 2×2 block of the source into one output texel.
    ///
    /// Sampled with a bilinear tap at the block's centre rather than four `Load`s: a bilinear filter
    /// *is* the average of the four texels it sits between, so the hardware does the reduction for
    /// free. That only holds when the source is exactly twice the target's size, which is what makes
    /// the halving chain a chain rather than an arbitrary resize.
    func Reduce(coord: int2) {
        val size = target.GetDimensions()

        // The dispatch is rounded up to whole groups, so the tail invocations have no texel. Storing
        // outside a storage image is undefined in both targets, which means a corrupted neighbour on
        // one driver and a device loss on another.
        if (coord.x >= size.x || coord.y >= size.y) {
            return
        }

        val centre = float2(float(coord.x) + 0.5f, float(coord.y) + 0.5f)
        val uv = centre / float2(float(size.x), float(size.y))
        val tap = source.SampleLevel(sourceSampler, uv, 0f)

        target.Store(coord, float4(FirstStep ? LogLuminance(tap.rgb) : tap.x, 0f, 0f, 0f))
    }

    /// Log luminance, floored so a black texel is a large negative number rather than minus infinity.
    ///
    /// The floor is what stops a single black pixel poisoning the whole average: `log(0)` is not a
    /// number, and one of those in a sum makes the sum not a number too — a frame that goes
    /// completely black and never recovers, which is the classic way this effect fails.
    func LogLuminance(color: float3): float {
        return log(max(ColorSpaces.Luminance(color), Const.Epsilon))
    }

    /// Eases the stored exposure toward what this frame wants.
    func Adapt() {
        if (exposure.Length < 1) {
            return
        }

        Ease(exp(average.Load(int2(0, 0)).x))
    }

    /// Moves the stored exposure toward the one a measured scene luminance asks for.
    ///
    /// Shared by both meters on purpose: the chain and the histogram disagree about *what the scene's
    /// luminance is* and about nothing else, so the bounds, the rates and the middle grey are one
    /// piece of arithmetic rather than two that can drift.
    func Ease(scene: float) {
        val target = clamp(middleGrey / max(scene, Const.Epsilon), minimumExposure, maximumExposure)
        val previous = exposure[0]

        // ⚠ The stored exposure is trusted only where it is a number this node could have
        // written, and one bit pattern is why. Nothing seeds the buffer — `AutoExposureRenderer`
        // says so, and the argument is that the first frame is handed a `deltaTime` of three hours
        // so its blend is one and whatever the allocation held is scaled to nothing. That is true of
        // every finite value and false for exactly one class: a lerp is `a + t*(b - a)`, and
        // `b - NaN` is `NaN` whatever `t` is. So the settle frame that was supposed to make the seed
        // irrelevant propagated it, and every frame after it eased from a NaN and stayed one.
        //
        // ⚠ Which reads as a driver bug and is not one. A fresh device-local allocation comes back
        // zeroed on MoltenVK — finite, so the settle frame worked and nobody could see the argument
        // was fragile — and does not on lavapipe, where sample 03 rendered a black frame from frame
        // one to frame sixty-four with every counter above it healthy. The bounds are the test rather
        // than `isnan` because they catch the whole class: NaN fails both comparisons since every
        // comparison against a NaN is false, and an infinity or a large finite leftover fails the
        // upper one. Every write below is inside the bounds, so nothing outside them is this node's.
        val trusted = previous >= minimumExposure && previous <= maximumExposure

        if (!trusted) {
            exposure[0] = target
            return
        }

        // Which rate applies depends on which way the exposure is moving, and "brightening the image"
        // is the eye going *dark*-adapted — the slow direction. Getting this the wrong way round is
        // easy and shows as walking out of a tunnel being instant while walking in takes seconds.
        val rate = target > previous ? darkenRate : brightenRate
        val blend = 1f - exp(-deltaTime * max(rate, Const.Epsilon))

        exposure[0] = lerp(previous, target, saturate(blend))
    }
}
